    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d62">

                <pb facs="0170" n="46" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg46"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A4/62: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harvey</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-07-27T18:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 27, 1921—6
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received July 27—4: 37
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>618. Supplementing the proposal just read to me by Curzon, which Geddes will hand
                    to you, the following is tentative British itinerary if you accept:</p>
                <p>Balfour and Curzon will attend August 4 meeting of Supreme Council at Paris.
                    …</p>
                <p>On August 12, Lloyd George, Curzon, Meighen,<anchor n="49" xml:id="d62-f1"/>
                    Hughes, and Massey to sail direct to Bar Harbor on a fast warship, arriving <pb
                        facs="0171" n="47" xml:id="pg47"/> August 18. They would remain one week
                    returning August 25 to arrive in England on August 31. If acceptable to you and
                    if housing arrangements can be made, they prefer Bar Harbor because as
                    contrasted with New York or other port they could save one day sailing each way.
                    Accommodations for the British delegation in cottages or hotels would be
                    required for 20 to 24 including secretaries, etc., and for 15 personal servants.
                    The Japanese, Curzon estimates, would require accommodations for 10 to 20. You
                    should of course determine accommodations for your delegation and number
                    comprising same. …</p>
                <p>This proposal goes today to Japan but clearly Curzon expects a favorable
                    response.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Harvey</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
